## RULE (1) -- the console window stays MINIMIZED and UNTOUCHED, and nobody clicks in it

Done 2026-10-13T06:5xZ, and the mechanism matters:
  runner PID 27560, MainWindowHandle 2359526
  BEFORE: IsIconic = False   (the window was open on the desktop -- one stray click away from the
                              exact state that stalled its predecessor for nine hours)
  ACT   : ShowWindow(2359526, SW_MINIMIZE=6)  rc=True
  AFTER : IsIconic = True

WHY THIS CALL AND NOT A CLICK: ShowWindow sets a WINDOW STATE. It injects no input, so unlike a
click or a keypress in the window it CANNOT create a mark/QuickEdit selection. The hazard and the
remedy run through the same window, so the remedy had to be the one that cannot cause the hazard.

CONTROL, because minimizing a console must not be the thing that stops it:
  title after : 'C:\Program Files\Python314\python.exe'  -- no 'Select ' prefix
  CPU 1.09375 before the minimize -> 1.125 after, and 3 threads throughout. Still accruing, so the
  minimize did not block the process.
This seat sends NO further input to that window. The only remaining way in is a human at the box,
which is what the rule's "let nobody click in it" addresses and this seat cannot enforce.

## RULE (2) -- read cycle_runner.log's last write once an hour; on >15 min while Running, file a row and do nothing else

Kept by a MONITOR, not a cron (launch arming is zero crons; a Monitor is not a loop). It polls every
300 s -- more often than hourly, which is strictly safer and costs nothing because it emits ONLY on a
condition that would change what this seat does:

  TRIP               log last write > 15 min old WHILE the task reads Running
                     -> the issuer's row-and-nothing-else case. NO key, NO kill.
  WINDOW-SELECT      the title begins 'Select ' again -- the wedge itself, recurred
  TASK-NOT-RUNNING   state != Running (the cycle may have ended, or the task stopped)
  CYCLE-RECORD       a .cycle.json newer than the 05:52:43Z start -> the CYCLE ledger leg's gate;
                     the watch then ENDS, which is its known terminal condition
  Each emits once per transition, with a CLEARED message if it recovers, so a persistent state does
  not spam and a recovery is not silent.

EVERY LIMB WAS EXERCISED BEFORE ARMING, because a watch that reads wrong is worse than no watch:
  log age            0 min against mtime 1791874986 -- the stat read works
  state|title parse  'Running|C:\Program Files\Python314\python.exe' split correctly on the pipe
  cycle records      0 newer than START, with TWO controls: the glob matches 7 .cycle.json files
                     (so the 0 is a real zero, not a glob miss), and the stopped cycle's own record
                     20261012T191641Z.cycle.json reads mtime 1791870360 against START 1791870763 --
                     it EXISTS and is correctly EXCLUDED, 403 s before the new runner began.
  The epoch comparison is deliberate: `find -newermt` takes LOCAL time on this device and would have
  mis-bucketed every record by four hours.

## WHAT THIS SEAT WILL NOT DO UNDER THIS RULE
No key to any window. No kill by name, PID or Stop-ScheduledTask. No prevention change (windowless
or QuickEdit-off launch) -- that is round 84's first cut, not this order's, and not this file's.
